__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.egg-info/
/dist/
/build/
.venv/
*.db
*.db-journal

# Secrets — never commit. The repo is now PUBLIC; these guard against a stray
# credential file or keyring vault being accidentally added.
.env
.env.*
*.enc
*.pem
*.key
keys.enc
salt

# Frontend build artifacts: node_modules is huge; dist/ is built locally
# per-machine by scripts/update.sh (pnpm install + pnpm build) and pinned to
# HEAD via dist/.commit-sha. No CI artifact distribution. Never commit dist/.
src/okuro/web/frontend/node_modules/
src/okuro/web/frontend/*.tsbuildinfo
src/okuro/web/dist/
# Prism single-file HTML export template — built by `pnpm build:export`.
src/okuro/web/export-dist/
# redline in-frame overlay IIFE — built by `pnpm build:overlay`, served at
# /api/redline/doc/{id}/v/{seq}/serve/{token}/__redline__/overlay.js.
src/okuro/web/dist-overlay/

# okuro cortex sidecars — derivative analysis, regenerated by daemon
.okuro-index.yaml
.agent_scan_marker

# Vendored embedding model — downloaded by hatch_build.py during wheel build,
# kept out of git to avoid a 130 MB commit.
src/okuro/embed/models/
/.install.log

# Local AI-agent dev artifacts (skills, worktrees)
.claude/
.playwright-mcp/

# Local eval run outputs — regenerated per run, not source of truth
scripts/cortex_eval/results/

# okuro worktree binding — per-tree local state, never committed
.okuro-worktree

# The one-folder layout. These are SIBLING checkouts and archives that live
# inside the repo directory for tidiness only — they are never part of the
# product and must never be staged.
#
#   worktrees/  branch-bound worktrees (`okuro wt add`)
#   release/    the generated release repo's clone — holds release commits
#   backups/    history-rewrite bundles and other archives
#
# Ignored, so `git status` stays about the product and the release export's
# dirty-surface check (which reads the working tree) cannot see them.
#
# NOTE these hold real, sometimes ONLY copies — release/ carries release
# commits that exist nowhere else until they are pushed. `git clean -x`
# targets ignored files by definition, so it would delete them. Nothing in
# okuro runs git clean (verified: zero occurrences in src/, scripts/,
# installer/, both installers, both bootstraps, .github/ and tests/), but a
# hand-typed one would.
/worktrees/
/release/
/backups/

# Parked work-in-progress — kept out of the build and out of git status.
# Anything here is deliberately not part of the app; see its own notes.
.scratch/

# ── The user's workbench ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# CONVENIENCE, NOT A GUARD. .gitignore does not untrack anything already in
# the index and does not survive `git add -f`, so it prevents nothing on its
# own. Its job here is to keep `git status` readable so a real addition is
# visible in the noise.
#
# The actual guard is the SCOPE invariant in `okuro setup-git-guards`, where
# the repo ROOT is an allowlist (PRODUCT_ROOT_ENTRIES in
# src/okuro/cli/cmd_git_guards.py) — that refuses these and every scratch
# directory a future session invents, which a list like this cannot. CI
# backstops both via tests/repo/test_no_personal_data_tracked.py.
#
# okuro ships the base design system and okuro-ds. Brands, stacks, design
# systems and personal work are the USER's data.
/.okuro/
/deliverables/
/design/
/tmp/
/prism-*-proof/
/b.js
/blank.html
/gallery-qa-v3-screenshot.png

# Deploy-hook snapshots — generated, never authored. The hook copies the live
# tree here before applying a commit, so whatever the repo contained at that
# moment reappears under a timestamped directory. Tracking it would commit a
# copy of the app to itself, and leaving it untracked left it as permanent
# `git status` noise.
.deploy-backups/

# A 5.8 GB Python virtualenv that ended up inside the repo — bin/ lib/ share/
# plus a NESTED COPY of okuro's own source at _linux-env/src/okuro/src/okuro/.
# Never tracked, and not deleted here: ignoring it was approved, removing it
# was not. Two costs it was imposing while merely untracked — 85 of the 114
# paths tests/test_no_stray_md.py rejects live in it (third-party LICENSE.md
# and README.md), and cortex indexes the nested source copy, so a code search
# returns the real file AND a stale duplicate at a different line number.
# Measured 2026-08-28: cortex_search_code("DELETE FROM agent_memory") returned
# src/okuro/daemon/_handlers.py:467 and _linux-env/.../_handlers.py:464.
/_linux-env/
